{"profile":{"name":"Mike Colle","ward":"Ward 8, Eglinton-Lawrence","bio":"Mike Colle has represented Ward 8, Eglinton-Lawrence on Toronto City Council since December 2018 and was re-elected in 2022 with 70.3% of the vote; he has served as Deputy Mayor for North York since August 2023. Before joining council he was the Liberal MPP for Eglinton-Lawrence from 1995 to 2018, serving as a cabinet minister under Premier Dalton McGuinty. Earlier in his career he was a councillor for the City of York and Metropolitan Toronto, TTC chair from 1988 to 1994, and a high school history and economics teacher.","committees":["Infrastructure and Environment Committee (Vice-Chair)","Executive Committee","North York Community Council","Corporations Nominating Panel (Vice-Chair)"],"contact":{"email":"councillor_colle8@toronto.ca","phone":"416-338-2500"},"promises":[{"text":"Increase the supply of affordable housing through the Lawrence Heights Revitalization, the Reena residence, the Shortt Street parking lot proposal, and inclusionary zoning in all new developments.","source":"https://www.cedarvaleuppervillage.com/blog/pending-city-of-toronto-2022-election-meet-the-candidates"},{"text":"Expand Mobile Mental Health Crisis Response Teams to respond to mental health and substance abuse emergencies, including fentanyl poisonings in the Eglinton-Marlee area.","source":"https://www.cedarvaleuppervillage.com/blog/pending-city-of-toronto-2022-election-meet-the-candidates"},{"text":"Increase neighbourhood policing to combat gun crimes, carjackings, hate crimes, and deal with traffic enforcement.","source":"https://www.cedarvaleuppervillage.com/blog/pending-city-of-toronto-2022-election-meet-the-candidates"}]},"lobbying":{"total":645,"term":290,"first":"2018-12-07","last":"2026-10-08","lobbyists":169,"clients":128,"subjects":281,"methods":{"Meetings":147,"Emails":393,"Phone calls":75,"Written":18,"Other or unreported":12},"top":[{"label":"Planning and Development Application, Zoning By-law","cou